# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Compression layer: Section 5 part A (5.1 - 5.5.1)

REVS = {
    # 5.1
    "P147": ("A theoretical framework is a set of assumptions and concepts, connected by an argument,",
             "A theoretical framework is a set of assumptions and concepts, connected by an argument, that determines what a study observes, how it interprets what it observes and what it will accept as an explanation. It does three kinds of work at once: it specifies an ontology, a view of what exists and what can act; an epistemology, a view of how knowledge about those things can be produced; and an axiology, a view of what the research is for and whose interests it serves."),
    "P148": ("The literature on doctoral research is consistent",
             "The literature on doctoral research is consistent about this function. Grant and Osanloo (2014) describe the theoretical framework as the blueprint of a dissertation: it determines the rooms that can be built and the connections between them. Ravitch and Riggan (2017) argue that a conceptual framework is not a diagram appended to a literature review but the researcher's reasoned account of why the study is designed as it is. Sandberg and Alvesson (2011) show that constructing a research question is itself a theoretical act, and Alvesson and K\u00e4rreman (2007) add that empirical material becomes interesting when it resists the theory used to generate it."),
    "P149": ("Four functions follow.",
             "Four functions follow. A framework prevents description from masquerading as analysis: a study that reports what people said, without a theory of why they said it, produces data rather than knowledge. It makes assumptions contestable, since an implicit assumption cannot be examined at all. It connects the study to a tradition, so that the reader can see which conversation is being joined and what would count as a contribution. And it disciplines interpretation: a researcher committed to a theory can be held to its implications, whereas a researcher who has not can explain any finding after the fact."),
    "P150": ("The relationship between theory and empirical work runs in both directions.",
             "The relationship between theory and empirical work runs in both directions: theory shapes questions, design and interpretation, while surprising evidence can revise theory \u2014 the logic of abduction (Alvesson and K\u00e4rreman, 2007). The surprise is not a nuisance; it is the point at which the study has something to say."),
    "P151": ("A theoretical framework is not the same as a conceptual framework,",
             "A theoretical framework is not the same as a conceptual framework, and the distinction matters for the structure of this proposal. The theoretical framework is the general body of theory the study adopts, abstract and portable; the conceptual framework is the researcher's own model of the specific problem, concrete and local. Section 5 establishes the theory; Section 6 constructs the model. A common weakness in applied research is to collapse the two, so that the model is presented as if it were a theory and the theory is never tested against anything."),
    "P152": ("The consequences of a weak or absent framework are predictable:",
             "The consequences of a weak or absent framework are predictable: variables selected ad hoc, unfalsifiable claims, findings that cannot be generalised because no theory says what they are a case of, and recommendations that follow the researcher's preferences rather than the analysis. In engineering management the pressure to be useful is strong, and a framework is sometimes dismissed as an academic ornament that delays practical work. The argument of this section is the opposite: without a framework, the practical work has no way of knowing what it is looking at."),
    # 5.2
    "P154": ("Engineering Management sits at the intersection",
             "Engineering Management sits at the intersection of technical systems, organisational behaviour, economics, innovation studies and public policy, and its problems are rarely confined to one level: a decision about a technology is also a decision about skills, contracts, maintenance, regulation and trust. A technically sound project can still fail because the operating organisation lacks capacity, the tariff makes the service unaffordable, or the community has learned from previous projects that promises are not kept."),
    "P155": ("The field has borrowed theories from several disciplines:",
             "The field has borrowed theories from several disciplines: TAM and its successors from psychology and information systems, Lean Thinking and the resource-based view from operations, complexity and systems thinking from complexity science, and socio-technical systems theory and the multi-level perspective from sociology and innovation studies. Each has been productive in its home setting. The difficulty arises when a theory developed for a firm, an individual or a market is applied to an infrastructural problem in a low-income settlement: the theory does not merely lose precision, it mis-specifies the object."),
    "P156": ("Three mis-specifications recur.",
             "Three mis-specifications recur. Level confusion: a theory of individual intention is used to explain a collective outcome, or of firm strategy to explain a household practice. Black-boxing: the artefact is treated as a fixed input whose properties are known, when its properties are part of what has to be explained. Context as background: what the model controls for rather than what it is about. In a study of energy in an informal settlement, context is the substance \u2014 tenure, density, infrastructure, income, gender and municipal capacity are not variables to be held constant but the conditions that make a technology work or fail."),
    "P157": ("A framework adequate to this problem therefore needs to be relational and multi-level,",
             "A framework adequate to this problem therefore needs to be relational and multi-level, to hold human and non-human elements in one frame without reducing one to the other, to be normative because the problem is about justice, and to be empirically tractable because the findings must be defensible. These requirements are the criteria applied in Section 5.11."),
    "P158": ("There is also a question of disciplinary fit.",
             "There is also the question of disciplinary fit. Engineering Management values usefulness: a theory that cannot inform a decision will not survive in the field however elegant it is. The framework is therefore assessed not only for philosophical coherence but for whether it changes what a design team would do on a Monday morning."),
    # 5.3.1
    "P161": ("Humanism is less a single doctrine than a family of commitments.",
             "Humanism is less a single doctrine than a family of commitments: the human being as the central subject of knowledge and value; culture separated from nature and mind from body; a universal human essence as the measure of other beings. These commitments enabled universal rights, scientific inquiry and the critique of arbitrary authority, but they also produced exclusions: the universal human was in practice male, European, propertied and able-bodied, and those who did not fit were treated as less than fully human \u2014 as nature rather than culture, as objects of study rather than participants in knowledge."),
    "P162": ("Posthumanism begins from this diagnosis.",
             "Posthumanism begins from this diagnosis. It does not reject humanism's achievements; it rejects the pretence that the human is a self-contained, universal subject, and asks what becomes visible when the pretence is dropped. The question is not \u201cwhat comes after the human?\u201d but \u201chow has the human been produced, and what else is involved in that production?\u201d"),
    # 5.3.2
    "P165": ("The first is posthumanism as a historical condition.",
             "The first is posthumanism as a historical condition: humans are increasingly entangled with technologies, data, infrastructures and ecologies, and the boundaries between body and machine, nature and culture, are harder to draw. Hayles (1999) and Braidotti (2013) describe the condition, although they evaluate it differently."),
    "P166": ("The second is critical posthumanism,",
             "The second is critical posthumanism, a normative and philosophical project drawing on anti-humanism, feminism, postcolonial thought and science and technology studies, which affirms difference rather than universalism and treats knowledge as situated. Braidotti (2013, 2019) is its most systematic contemporary exponent; Wolfe (2010) distinguishes it carefully from the third formation."),
    "P167": ("The third is transhumanism,",
             "The third is transhumanism, which seeks to enhance human capacities through technology \u2014 longer life, greater intelligence, control over the body. Its values remain humanist: perfectibility, mastery, transcendence. This study rejects transhumanism, and not as a matter of taste: transhumanism would direct attention to enhancing individual capacities and away from the collective, material and institutional arrangements that are the subject of the study."),
    "P168": ("A fourth position, technological determinism, is also rejected.",
             "A fourth position, technological determinism, is also rejected. The alternative adopted here is not that society is autonomous but that technology and society are co-constituted: a prepaid meter does not cause a particular relation between landlord and tenant; it materialises a relation already being negotiated, and in doing so changes the terms of the negotiation."),
    # 5.3.3
    "P170": ("The central ontological claim of critical posthumanism is that entities do not precede their relations.",
             "The central ontological claim of critical posthumanism is that entities do not precede their relations. In Barad's (2007) agential realism the primary unit is the phenomenon, not the object: an entanglement of matter and meaning in which boundaries are enacted. The relata within a phenomenon acquire their properties through intra-action; they do not exist first and then interact. Intra-action is the relation through which they emerge."),
    "P171": ("This is a difficult claim, and an example may make it concrete.",
             "The claim is difficult, and easy to repeat without understanding. A biogas digester is not a thing with fixed properties introduced into a household. What the digester is, in practice, depends on the feedstock available, the water that can be spared, the space allocated, the stove used, the person who maintains it and the agreements that govern it. Change the water supply and the digester becomes a different object, possibly a burden rather than a service. The properties that matter are not intrinsic to the vessel; they are produced in the relation."),
    "P172": ("Haraway's (1988) epistemology is a companion to this ontology.",
             "Haraway's (1988) epistemology is a companion to this ontology. Knowledge is always from somewhere: the \u201cview from nowhere\u201d conceals a position rather than escaping one. Situated knowledge is objective in a different way, because it can be located and checked. The implication is direct: a resident's knowledge of how a stove behaves in a small kitchen is not anecdote awaiting expert validation; it is knowledge produced at a particular site, indispensable to a design that has to work there."),
    # 5.3.4
    "P174": ("If entities emerge through relations, then agency cannot be a property",
             "If entities emerge through relations, agency cannot belong to humans alone. Barad (2007) describes agency as an enactment rather than an attribute, distributed across the apparatus through which a phenomenon is observed and the materials that participate in it. The agential cut is the boundary drawn within a phenomenon between what is observed and what observes; it is not arbitrary, but it is a cut \u2014 it includes and excludes, and it has consequences."),
    "P175": ("Latour's (2005) actor-network theory is adjacent to this position",
             "Latour's (2005) actor-network theory is adjacent but not identical: humans and non-humans act symmetrically as actants, and the networks through which action is assembled can be traced. Actor-network theory is empirically useful because it shows how a technology enrols allies and how it can be undone; it is less useful for normative questions, because it is deliberately indifferent to the justice of the network it describes. The study uses Latour's sensibility without adopting his ontology wholesale."),
    "P176": ("Bennett's (2010) notion of thing-power is also relevant.",
             "Bennett's (2010) thing-power is also relevant: matter has a vitality of its own, and things can resist, surprise and exceed human intention. A digester that produces too little gas, a clogged pipe, a stove that will not sit flat on a pot \u2014 these are moments in which the material world asserts its own agency and changes what the household can do."),
    "P177": ("The consequence for research is significant.",
             "The consequence for research is significant: a study that asks only what people think has already excluded most of what is going on. The meter, the fuel, the dwelling, the water supply and the maintenance arrangement are not context; they are participants."),
    # 5.3.5
    "P179": ("Posthumanism takes matter seriously,",
             "Posthumanism takes matter seriously, which distinguishes it from constructionism's willingness to treat the material world as a product of discourse. Matter is not passive: a prepaid meter is not a neutral measuring device but enacts a relation between household, utility and money \u2014 it requires purchase before use, it can disconnect a household automatically, and it shifts cash-flow management onto the household. Those material effects shape practice, and they are not captured by asking whether the meter is easy to use."),
    "P180": ("A biogas digester is another example.",
             "A digester is another example. Its material requirements \u2014 water, feedstock, temperature, space \u2014 produce social requirements: someone must collect the waste, carry the water, be available when the system needs attention, and be able to buy a spare part. The technology distributes labour, usually along gendered lines. That is a property of the artefact-in-relation, not a social consequence appended to a technical artefact."),
    # 5.3.6
    "P182": ("Posthumanism also insists on the interdependence",
             "Posthumanism also insists on the interdependence of human and ecological systems: the nature\u2013culture distinction is a way of organising the world, not a description of it, and the organisation has costs. The Anthropocene names a condition in which human activity has become a geological force and its consequences are unevenly distributed \u2014 those who contributed least to emissions are often most exposed to their effects."),
    "P183": ("For this study, ecological interdependence matters in two ways.",
             "Ecological interdependence matters here in two ways. Energy interventions have environmental effects that are not externalities to be added at the end: a biogas system that consumes water in a water-scarce settlement is not environmentally benign simply because it is renewable. And environmental and social justice are not separate agendas: the arrangements that expose residents to smoke and fire also expose them to floods, heat and pollution. A framework that treats the social and the environmental as separate domains will miss the connection."),
    # 5.3.7
    "P185": ("Posthumanist ethics is relational and affirmative.",
             "Posthumanist ethics is relational and affirmative. Braidotti (2006, 2013) contrasts potestas, the power that negates and constrains, with potentia, the capacity that enables and creates \u2014 a distinction that avoids the assumption that power is always repressive. A community can have the capacity to organise and build even without formal authority; the task of a just intervention is to increase potentia without ignoring the potestas that constrains it."),
    "P186": ("Haraway (2016) offers the concept of response-ability:",
             "Haraway (2016) offers response-ability: the practised, accountable capacity to respond to others, human and non-human. Barad (2007) describes ethics as ethico-onto-epistemology: what we choose to observe, and how, is already an ethical decision, because questions of being and knowing cannot be separated from questions of value."),
    "P187": ("These ideas have practical implications for a Living Lab.",
             "These ideas have practical implications for a Living Lab. Who is invited to the first meeting, which data are recorded, who is credited for an innovation, and who bears the risk of failure are all ethical decisions. A framework that treats ethics as a consent form signed at the start will miss most of them."),
    # 5.4
    "P196": ("Braidotti supplies the normative horizon.",
             "Braidotti supplies the normative horizon. Her critique of humanist universalism is the reason the study cannot treat \u201cthe user\u201d as a generic category, her affirmative ethics the reason the framework aims at capacity-building rather than deficit-repair, and her potestas/potentia distinction (Braidotti, 2006), used by Aboalez (2025), a way of talking about power that is not only negative. But her work is not a method: it does not tell the researcher what to observe in a settlement. The study uses her concepts and resists her prose."),
    "P197": ("Haraway supplies the epistemology.",
             "Haraway supplies the epistemology. Situated knowledge is the strongest available alternative to the assumption that the designer knows best, and response-ability describes the ethical relation between researchers and residents more demandingly than informed consent. Her later concept of sympoiesis, making-with (Haraway, 2016), is directly relevant to a Living Lab because it treats collaboration as a condition of life rather than a technique. The limitation is that Haraway offers figures and provocations, not procedures."),
    "P198": ("Barad supplies the ontology and the analytical unit.",
             "Barad supplies the ontology and the analytical unit. Intra-action is the reason the study treats the phenomenon, not the user or the artefact, as the unit of analysis; the apparatus is the reason a Living Lab can be understood as producing the phenomenon it studies; and diffraction \u2014 reading insights through one another \u2014 holds the interview, observational and workshop material together in the analysis. The risk is obscurity, since Barad's vocabulary is drawn from quantum physics; every technical term in this proposal is therefore defined in plain English and illustrated with an energy example."),
    "P199": ("Hayles supplies the corrective to abstraction.",
             "Hayles supplies the corrective to abstraction. Her account of embodiment insists that information is always instantiated in a body, a device and a material arrangement, and her concept of technogenesis \u2014 humans and technologies co-evolving \u2014 supports the refusal to treat technology as an external object. Her work is less useful for justice and participation, and is not asked to carry that weight."),
    "P200": ("The four thinkers converge on a rejection",
             "The four thinkers converge on a rejection of the autonomous humanist subject, on relationality and on the critique of the nature\u2013culture divide; they diverge on almost everything else \u2014 Braidotti a vitalist monist, Barad a realist about relations, Haraway a situated pragmatist, Hayles a media theorist of embodiment. The study does not present them as one theory. It assembles a small number of concepts from each, states why each is needed, and accepts the assembly as a construction rather than a discovery."),
    "P201": ("This is also the point at which the study connects",
             "This is also where the study connects to the supervisor's work. Aboalez (2025) draws on Braidotti and Barad to argue for a posthumanist social approach to energy transition. The present study extends that argument from engineering education to settlement-level design, adding Haraway's epistemology and Hayles's materialism to address questions the earlier paper did not have to answer."),
    # 5.5
    "P203": ("An energy system is usually described as a chain:",
             "An energy system is usually described as a chain: generation, transmission, distribution, metering, appliances, end use. The description is accurate as far as it goes, but it separates the technical from the social and treats the social as where the technical is received. A posthumanist account describes the same system as an assemblage: a configuration of people, materials, infrastructures, institutions, fuels, dwellings, meters, tariffs and practices whose relations produce the capacity to deliver an energy service."),
    "P204": ("The difference is not merely semantic.",
             "The difference is not semantic. In the chain description the household is the end point; in the assemblage description it is one element among many, and the service is produced by the relations between them. A cooking service is produced by the stove, the fuel, the pot, the kitchen, the cook, the money that buys the fuel, the tariff, the reliability of supply and the norms governing what counts as a proper meal. Change any one of these and the service changes."),
    "P205": ("This way of thinking has become more common in energy social science.",
             "This way of thinking has become more common in energy social science. Sovacool (2014) calls for an agenda that takes infrastructure, practice and politics seriously, and the multi-level perspective describes how niches, regimes and landscapes interact over time (Geels, 2002, 2011). That literature explains why transitions are slow and why a promising niche can fail to displace an incumbent regime, but it operates at a high level of aggregation: it can explain the fate of a technology category, less well why one digester works and the next does not."),
    "P206": ("The value of posthumanism is that it operates at the scale",
             "Posthumanism's value is that it works at the scale of the configuration without losing the wider structures: the tariff, the by-law, the landlord's rule, the stove and the pot are elements of the same assemblage even though they operate at different scales."),
    # 5.5.1
    "P208": ("Consider a community-scale biogas digester proposed for an informal settlement.",
             "Consider a community-scale digester proposed for an informal settlement. A conventional engineering assessment would specify feedstock, hydraulic retention time, volume, gas yield and capital cost; a TAM study would add measures of perceived usefulness and ease of use. Both produce useful information; neither explains what happens next. A posthumanist analysis asks a different set of questions."),
    "P209": ("Feedstock and water. Who produces the organic waste,",
             "Feedstock and water. Who produces the organic waste, who collects it, and what else do they do with their time? If the waste is sold or fed to animals, the digester competes with an existing use; if collection falls to women or children, the labour cost falls on them; if the supply is seasonal, the output will be seasonal. Digestion also needs water in a fixed ratio to feedstock, and in a settlement where water is shared, purchased or carried, the digester competes with cooking, cleaning and drinking. A design that assumes a tap in the yard has already excluded the households most in need."),
    "P210": ("Space, temperature and cooking. A digester needs a footprint,",
             "Space, temperature and cooking. A digester needs a footprint, a slope, access for maintenance and a safe distance from dwellings, and in a dense settlement space is claimed and contested, so siting is a decision about whose space is taken and who is compensated. Microbial activity depends on temperature, so a system that performs in a warm climate may underperform in winter \u2014 a material property that shapes the service that can be promised, not a detail for a safety factor. And gas burns differently from wood or paraffin: the flame is less visible, the heat more concentrated, the pot may need to change. Ignore the pot and the dish, and the cook will judge the system unusable whatever the gas yield."),
    "P211": ("Maintenance, labour and institutions. A digester needs someone",
             "Maintenance, labour and institutions. A digester needs someone with time, tools, training and a reason to keep going to check the feed, remove the digestate, clear a blockage and replace a valve; if the arrangement depends on a volunteer who moves away, the technology has a design fault, not a social problem. The labour is usually assigned to women, so a system that increases that labour while reducing smoke may be good on one dimension and bad on another \u2014 the frame makes the distribution visible by treating labour as part of the assemblage. A permit, a waste agreement, a municipal connection or a credit arrangement may also be required: these are not \u201cenabling environment\u201d factors outside the project; their presence or absence changes what the technology is."),
    "P212": ("None of this is invisible to a good engineer.",
             "None of this is invisible to a good engineer: practitioners know that feedstock, water, space and maintenance decide whether a system survives. The theory's contribution is not the list of factors but the claim that they are constitutive of the technology rather than external to it, so that the unit of design is the configuration. If the claim is accepted, prototyping must include the meter, the tariff, the dwelling, the maintenance contract and the waste supply \u2014 the arrangement, not only the device, is what gets evaluated."),
    "P213": ("A TAM study would report that residents find the idea of biogas useful.",
             "A TAM study would report that residents find the idea of biogas useful. A posthumanist study would report that the system will fail where water is carried, succeed where a market provides a steady waste stream, and add a burden for the women expected to feed it. The second account is less tidy, but more useful for design."),
}
